### Types of Cloud
I learned about three common cloud deployment models:

#### Public Cloud
Public cloud services are shared infrastructure provided over the internet by a cloud provider.

They are commonly used because they are flexible, scalable, and cost-effective.

#### Private Cloud
A private cloud is dedicated to one organisation.

It provides greater control, customisation, and security for sensitive workloads.

#### Hybrid Cloud
A hybrid cloud combines public and private cloud environments.

This allows organisations to keep sensitive systems private while also using public cloud resources when more capacity is needed.

### Cloud Service Models
I learned about the three main cloud service models:

#### Infrastructure as a Service – IaaS
IaaS provides basic computing resources such as:

- Virtual machines
- Storage
- Networking

The customer manages the operating system and applications, while the cloud provider manages the physical hardware.

#### Platform as a Service – PaaS
PaaS provides a ready-to-use platform for developing and running applications.

The cloud provider manages the infrastructure and operating system, allowing developers to focus mainly on the application.

#### Software as a Service – SaaS
SaaS provides complete software applications over the internet.

Users access the software through a browser or app without managing the underlying infrastructure.

Examples include services such as Gmail and Zoom.

